## SCENARIO QUESTIONS

---

**Q13. SCENARIO: Your team says "we can just use a Deployment with a PVC for our PostgreSQL database -- we don't need StatefulSet." Under what circumstances are they RIGHT, and when are they WRONG?**

They are RIGHT for a `replicas: 1` (single-instance) setup. With
only one pod, there is no "multiple pods sharing the same disk"
problem. The single pod mounts a PVC created separately (not via
volumeClaimTemplates), and if the pod crashes, Deployment recreates
it pointing at that same pre-existing PVC — data survives. This
pattern is genuinely used for development databases, small internal
tools, or non-critical single instances.

They are WRONG the moment they need `replicas: 2` or more. All
replicas would reference the SAME PVC — multiple database processes
writing to the same data files simultaneously. Most storage backends
reject this at the volume-attachment level for `ReadWriteOnce` PVCs
(pods get stuck `ContainerCreating`), and even where technically
allowed, it causes immediate data corruption. StatefulSet's
`volumeClaimTemplates` solves this by giving each replica its own
separate PVC. Additionally, Deployment can't provide stable DNS names
for replicas to discover and connect to each other by ordinal.

---

**Q14. SCENARIO: You have a 3-replica StatefulSet for PostgreSQL. `postgres-0` is the primary. It gets OOMKilled and restarts. During the brief period it's restarting, what happens to read traffic being sent to `postgres-1` and `postgres-2`?**

`postgres-1` and `postgres-2` are unaffected — StatefulSet restarts
are per-pod and do NOT cause other pods to restart or lose their
own storage. Read traffic to `postgres-1` and `postgres-2` continues
normally. However, WRITE traffic directed to `postgres-0` (the
primary) will fail during the restart window — any write requests
from the application will get connection refused or timeout errors
until `postgres-0` comes back up and is Ready.

This is exactly why production database clusters at scale use a tool
like Patroni — during `postgres-0`'s restart, Patroni detects the
primary is gone and PROMOTES `postgres-1` to primary, redirecting
write traffic there. Without Patroni (bare StatefulSet only),
writes are down until `postgres-0` recovers.

