**Q15. SCENARIO: Your StatefulSet has `replicas: 3`. You update the pod template to use a new image. The rollout seems to be going very slowly — only one pod has updated after 10 minutes. Is this a problem?**

Not necessarily — this is expected behavior for StatefulSet with
the default `updateStrategy.rollingUpdate` and
`podManagementPolicy: OrderedReady`. StatefulSet rolls out updates
in REVERSE ordinal order (highest first: updates `postgres-2`, waits
for it to be Ready, then `postgres-1`, waits, then `postgres-0` last)
AND it is BLOCKING — it will not proceed to the next pod until the
current one is fully Running AND Ready.

If the new pod takes a long time to become Ready (slow startup,
readiness probe delay, image pull time), the entire rollout pauses
at that step.

```bash
kubectl rollout status sts/<name>
# Shows which pod is currently being updated and whether it's stuck

kubectl describe pod <new-pod-name>
# Check Events for ImagePullBackOff, readiness probe failures, etc.
```

If a pod is genuinely stuck (e.g., new image is broken), the rollout
will block indefinitely rather than continuing to break more pods —
this is protective behavior. Roll back with:
```bash
kubectl rollout undo sts/<name>
```

